  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14205615/assets/adcf36236abe7d2e691f8341857c9ef19b12821d7e952de0521d1877f510f326.png)\n\nThe figure above shows part of the graph of $y=f(x)$ which is defined on the interval $x\\in [-2,2].$ Given that $f(x)$ is odd, which of the following gives the complete graph of $y=f(x)?$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that the graph of an odd function must have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\nSo, we can obtain the missing part of the graph by rotating the given part ${180}^{∘}$ over the origin. \n\nTherefore, the complete graph of the function is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14205615/assets/f096c9cb18025c79f62fc1a8d700fc920e8cfb2c71040a0c03cc2da8b8346c20.png)",
